SATIN-ACCT-002.text:
- `SATIN-ACCT-002` Each item MUST be counted at this point:
  - the body, before any frame;
  - the applied authorities' records, before the first frame ([EIP-7702 Authorizations Before the First Frame](authorizations.md));
  - a frame start's records and transfer log, when the start is decided (`SATIN-LIM-013`);
  - a storage write's record, a log, and a `SELFDESTRUCT`'s records and transfer log, once the opcode completed;
  - deployed code, once every deposit charge is made and before the creation commits ([Gas Schedule and Intrinsic Gas](gas-schedule.md));
  - an Oracle hint's input, before the hint is forwarded, on the transaction and on no frame's budget; a hint that would cross the transaction's data-size limit is neither counted nor forwarded ([Oracle](../system-contracts/oracle.md)).

SATIN-ACCT-014.text:
- `SATIN-ACCT-014` The rises MUST be exactly:
  - a fresh slot, at the `SSTORE` transition of `SATIN-ACCT-016` that rises;
  - a value `CALL` to an account empty under EIP-161;
  - a `SELFDESTRUCT` that moves a non-zero balance to a distinct account empty under EIP-161 ([SELFDESTRUCT and the Destruction Table](selfdestruct.md));
  - the recipient of a transaction's value, when it is empty under EIP-161;
  - an applied EIP-7702 authorization whose authority the state holds no account for;
  - a `CREATE`, a `CREATE2`, a creation transaction or a keyless creation, when the created address is empty under EIP-161;
  - a keyless signer's account, when the creation's nonce bump creates it, counted on the keyless call ([KeylessDeploy](../system-contracts/keyless-deploy.md));
  - the caller account of a deposit, a Mega System Transaction executed as a deposit included, when that caller is empty under EIP-161, once (`SATIN-ACCT-020`, `SATIN-DEP-003`).

  Each is decided by the predicates of `SATIN-STATE-011`: an authority by existence — the state holds no account at its address — and every other site by EIP-161 emptiness — no account, or nonce zero, zero balance and no code.

SATIN-AUTH-004.text:
- `SATIN-AUTH-004` The entry's nonce MUST then be compared with the authority's effective nonce:
  - when an earlier entry of the list for the same authority was accepted, the simulated nonce after those acceptances;
  - otherwise, when the authority is the sender, the sender's nonce after validation's bump, so an authorization signed for the sender's nonce before the transaction does not match;
  - otherwise, the authority's current nonce.

  A nonce that differs MUST skip the entry; a nonce that matches accepts it, and the simulated nonce MUST then become one higher.

SATIN-AUTH-008.text:
- `SATIN-AUTH-008` The totals the list would keep, were it applied, MUST be:
  - one write record for each distinct accepted authority other than the sender, whose account is a fixed record of the body (`SATIN-ACCT-008`), however many of its entries are accepted;
  - one unit of state growth for each distinct accepted authority whose account does not exist — the state holds no account at its address, an account empty under EIP-161 counting as existing (`SATIN-STATE-011`) — however many of its entries are accepted;
  - the new-account state gas once for each such new authority.

SATIN-BLK-013.text:
- `SATIN-BLK-013` A transaction MUST be skipped for the current block, and MAY be included in a later one, when:
  - its declared gas limit does not fit in the block's remaining gas;
  - its data-availability footprint does not fit in what the block's gas limit has left for footprints (see [Base Layer](../base-layer.md));
  - it is not a deposit and the block has reached one of its packing budgets;
  - it is not a deposit and the block admits only deposits (see [Base Layer](../base-layer.md)).

  A block that contains a transaction these rules skip MUST be invalid.

SATIN-DET-001.text:
- `SATIN-DET-001` A read of each of the following MUST be a volatile read:
  - the block environment: `NUMBER`, `TIMESTAMP`, `COINBASE`, `PREVRANDAO`, `GASLIMIT`, `BASEFEE`, `BLOBBASEFEE`, `SLOTNUM` and `BLOCKHASH`;
  - the block beneficiary's account, through `BALANCE`, `SELFBALANCE`, `EXTCODESIZE`, `EXTCODECOPY`, `EXTCODEHASH`, the four call opcodes and the EIP-7702 delegate they follow, `SELFDESTRUCT` at either end, a transaction's sender or call recipient that is the beneficiary, a recipient that delegates to it, an applied EIP-7702 authority that is the beneficiary, and a keyless deployment's signer that is the beneficiary ([KeylessDeploy](../system-contracts/keyless-deploy.md));
  - the Oracle's storage, through `SLOAD` in the Oracle's own frame ([Oracle](../system-contracts/oracle.md)).

SATIN-DET-021.text:
- `SATIN-DET-021` A call to a precompile MUST make its own regular charges first, as any opcode's — its static cost, its memory and the value transfer; every precompile of the set is warm from the transaction's start (`SATIN-SCHED-012`), so the call makes no cold access — and one that crosses MUST stop the calling frame before the price is consulted and before anything moves (`SATIN-LIM-010`), the opcode's charges coming before every decision about the start (`SATIN-HIST-023`).
  A call to a precompile forwarded more regular gas than the frame's allowance — what the limit leaves the transaction at the call — MUST then be decided from the precompile's price, read before it runs, for the precompile and input of the called address (`SATIN-PCMP-008`):
  - priced within the allowance, it runs on its whole forward;
  - priced past the allowance and within its forward, it MUST NOT run and is the crossing;
  - priced past its whole forward, it runs on its forward and fails, as without the read.

  The forward the price is held against is the child's whole regular gas (`SATIN-FWD-003`), a value call's `CALL_STIPEND` included, as `SATIN-DET-011` and `SATIN-STOP-014` count it.

SATIN-DET-023.text:
- `SATIN-DET-023` Every precompile of the precompile set (`SATIN-PCMP-001`, [Precompiles](precompiles.md)) MUST have a price for every input, which is the gas the precompile charges for that input before any check it makes after its gas charge:
  - an input over an OP precompile's size limit (`SATIN-PCMP-004`) is priced at zero, since it is refused before any gas check;
  - a ModExp input is priced by its Osaka formula whatever its lengths, an input over EIP-7823's bound included, since ModExp charges that price before it checks the bound (`SATIN-PCMP-007`).

SATIN-HIST-010.text:
- `SATIN-HIST-010` A value `CALL` to an account empty under EIP-161 MUST charge its caller in this order:
  1. its regular gas: the access's static cost, memory, the value transfer, then the cold surcharge with the new account's regular gas, which is zero ([Gas Schedule and Intrinsic Gas](gas-schedule.md));
  2. the new account's state gas: its legacy share from regular gas (`SATIN-STATE-012`), then the reservoir, then the rest from regular gas;
  3. the forward of the regular gas left ([Gas Forwarding](gas-forwarding.md));
  4. the history of the start's records.

  The legacy share, and a state charge's part the reservoir cannot pay, therefore shrink the forward, and the records' history does not; the forward is regular gas only.
  The child MUST be given its reservoir share after the history charge, from the reservoir that charge left the caller (`SATIN-POOL-011`).

SATIN-HIST-016.text:
- `SATIN-HIST-016` These MUST NOT pay history gas:
  - an [EIP-7708 transfer log](transfer-logs.md), which is data size only;
  - an Oracle hint's payload, which goes to the oracle service and not into a block ([Oracle](../system-contracts/oracle.md));
  - any byte of a system call, a pre-block call ([Mega System Transactions and the Protocol's Own Transactions](../system-contracts/system-tx.md)); for it the deposited-code history gas MUST be zero too.

  A deposit and a system-originated transaction, a Mega System Transaction among them, MUST pay history gas as any transaction does: for their body, their calldata, their logs, their write records and their deposited code.

SATIN-ICPT-006.text:
- `SATIN-ICPT-006` A `keylessDeploy(bytes,uint256)` call MUST be recognized by its depth (0, the transaction's own frame), its scheme (`CALL`), its target (`KEYLESS_DEPLOY_ADDRESS`), its selector, and its arguments, whatever code the state holds at the address; what such a call does is specified in [KeylessDeploy](keyless-deploy.md).
  The arguments MUST be accepted exactly when KeylessDeploy's own compiled calldata decoder for `(bytes calldata, uint256)` accepts them, that is when the input after the selector, each word read as a 32-byte big-endian integer, meets all of the following:
  1. it holds a head of at least 64 bytes, two 32-byte words;
  2. the head's first word, the offset of the `bytes`, is below 2^64;
  3. the word that starts at that offset, the length of the `bytes`, is below 2^64;
  4. offset + 32 + length is at most the length of the input after the selector; the `bytes` are the length bytes that follow the length word;
  5. nothing more is required: the `bytes` need no padding to a whole word, the offset need not be the canonical 64 and may point into the head, trailing bytes after the encoding are allowed, and the head's second word, `gasLimitOverride`, may hold any value.

  A call with the selector whose arguments the decoder refuses is not recognized: the node charges it nothing, and it runs KeylessDeploy's bytecode, as every other call to KeylessDeploy does; the bytecode's decoder refuses the same arguments, so the call reverts with empty data.

SATIN-KEY-001.text:
- `SATIN-KEY-001` KeylessDeploy MUST be the account at `KEYLESS_DEPLOY_ADDRESS` holding the code `KEYLESS_DEPLOY_CODE_HASH` (see [System Contracts](overview.md)), whose interface is:

```solidity
interface IKeylessDeploy {
    error MalformedEncoding();
    error NotContractCreation();
    error NotPreEIP155();
    error NonZeroTxNonce(uint64 txNonce);
    error NoEtherTransfer();
    error InvalidSignature();
    error InsufficientBalance();
    error ContractAlreadyExists();
    error SignerNonceTooHigh(uint64 signerNonce);
    error ExecutionReverted(uint64 gasUsed, bytes output);
    error ExecutionHalted(uint64 gasUsed);
    error EmptyCodeDeployed(uint64 gasUsed);
    error NoContractCreated();
    error AddressMismatch();
    error GasLimitTooLow(uint64 txGasLimit, uint64 providedGasLimit);
    error InitCodeTooLarge(uint64 size, uint64 max);
    error SignerHasCode();
    error NotIntercepted();

    function keylessDeploy(bytes calldata keylessDeploymentTransaction, uint256 gasLimitOverride)
        external
        returns (uint64 gasUsed, address deployedAddress, bytes memory errorData);
}
```

  Errors are ABI-encoded as Solidity custom errors: the four-byte selector of the signature, then the arguments.

SATIN-KEY-014.text:
- `SATIN-KEY-014` Unless a limit stopped it (`SATIN-KEY-016`), the call MUST succeed and return `(gasUsed, deployedAddress, errorData)`:
  - when the deploy address holds code afterwards: the deploy address and empty `errorData`;
  - otherwise the zero address and, as `errorData`, `ExecutionReverted(gasUsed, output)` for a creation that reverted (its revert data as `output`), `ExecutionHalted(gasUsed)` for one that halted, or `EmptyCodeDeployed(gasUsed)` for one `SATIN-KEY-009` refused or whose constructor destroyed the account.

SATIN-MAC-001.text:
- `SATIN-MAC-001` MegaAccessControl MUST be the account at `MEGA_ACCESS_CONTROL_ADDRESS` holding the code `MEGA_ACCESS_CONTROL_CODE_HASH` (see [System Contracts](overview.md)), whose interface is:

```solidity
interface IMegaAccessControl {
    enum VolatileDataAccessType {
        BlockNumber,  // 0
        Timestamp,    // 1
        Coinbase,     // 2
        Difficulty,   // 3
        GasLimit,     // 4
        BaseFee,      // 5
        PrevRandao,   // 6
        BlockHash,    // 7
        BlobBaseFee,  // 8
        BlobHash,     // 9
        Beneficiary,  // 10
        Oracle        // 11
    }

    error NotIntercepted();
    error NonZeroTransfer();
    error VolatileDataAccessDisabled(VolatileDataAccessType accessType);
    error DisabledByParent();

    function disableVolatileDataAccess() external view;
    function enableVolatileDataAccess() external view;
    function isVolatileDataAccessDisabled() external view returns (bool disabled);
}
```

  All three methods are intercepted for a `CALL` or a `STATICCALL`, under the rules of [Call Interception](interception.md), its value policy included (`SATIN-ICPT-003`); a `DELEGATECALL` or `CALLCODE`, and any other selector, run the bytecode, whose fallback reverts with `NotIntercepted()`.
  The methods change only an execution-local switch, the same under `CALL` and `STATICCALL`: they write no account or storage, emit no log and move no value.
  The switch is held per transaction, for the frame that made the call — the caller, one level above the frame the call would start:
  - `disableVolatileDataAccess()` switches volatile-data access off for the caller's frame and every frame below it; a switch already off from a frame above the caller stays as it is.
  - `enableVolatileDataAccess()` switches access back on when the switch was set by the caller's own frame or is not set, and succeeds; when a frame above the caller set it, the call MUST revert with `DisabledByParent()` and change nothing.
  - `isVolatileDataAccessDisabled()` returns `true` when access is off for the caller's frame, set by that frame or by a frame above it, and `false` otherwise.
  - The switch MUST turn back on when the frame that set it returns, whatever it returns with, so a frame it calls afterwards at the same level is not restricted.
  - A call that is the transaction's own frame has no caller frame: `disableVolatileDataAccess()` succeeds and switches nothing, `enableVolatileDataAccess()` succeeds, and `isVolatileDataAccessDisabled()` returns `false`.

SATIN-MLC-001.text:
- `SATIN-MLC-001` MegaLimitControl MUST be the account at `MEGA_LIMIT_CONTROL_ADDRESS` holding the code `MEGA_LIMIT_CONTROL_CODE_HASH` (see [System Contracts](overview.md)), whose interface is:

```solidity
interface IMegaLimitControl {
    error NotIntercepted();
    error NonZeroTransfer();

    function remainingComputeGas() external view returns (uint64 remaining);
}
```

  `remainingComputeGas()` is intercepted for a `CALL` or a `STATICCALL`, under the rules of [Call Interception](interception.md), its value policy included (`SATIN-ICPT-003`); a `DELEGATECALL` or `CALLCODE`, and any other selector, run the bytecode, whose fallback reverts with `NotIntercepted()`.

SATIN-MLC-002.text:
- `SATIN-MLC-002` `remainingComputeGas()` MUST return, ABI-encoded as a `uint64`, `remaining = min(own, allowance)`, read when the call reaches the interceptor, where:
  - `own` is the calling frame's own regular gas after the call deducted its forward, plus the gas the call forwarded, counted back;
  - `allowance` is, once a read of volatile data set a compute limit for the transaction, what that limit leaves the transaction at the start of the call's frame (`limit − compute`, zero once the compute is past the limit; see [Gas Detention](../evm/gas-detention.md)), and is absent, leaving `remaining = own`, while no limit is set.

SATIN-MST-008.text:
- `SATIN-MST-008` A system-originated transaction and every system call:
  - MUST be priced at the multiplier `m = 1` and MUST NOT read a SALT bucket's capacity (see [State Gas and SALT Pricing](../evm/state-gas.md));
  - MUST NOT be stopped by any per-transaction limit or frame budget, in data size, KV updates or state growth (see [Resource Limits](../evm/resource-limits.md));
  - MUST NOT be detained, whatever they read (see [Gas Detention](../evm/gas-detention.md));
  - MUST still have their usage counted in every dimension.

  A system call MUST also pay no history gas, its deposited code included; a system-originated transaction MUST pay history gas as any transaction does, its body, its calldata, its logs, its write records and its deposited code (see [History Gas](../evm/history-gas.md)).

SATIN-OUT-001.text:
- `SATIN-OUT-001` Every transaction a node is given MUST end in exactly one of these classes:
  - **rejected** before inclusion, when it fails validation and is not a deposit ([Transaction Validation and Refusals](../block/transaction-validation.md));
  - **included as a failed deposit** ([Deposit Transactions](../block/deposits.md));
  - **executed** to a success, a revert, a halt, or a limit stop of its own frame;
  - **no outcome**, when the node meets a fault (`SATIN-OUT-015`).

SATIN-PBLK-003.text:
- `SATIN-PBLK-003` Before its first transaction, a block MUST, in this order:
  1. make the EIP-2935 call to `HISTORY_STORAGE_ADDRESS` with the parent block's hash;
  2. make the EIP-4788 call to `BEACON_ROOTS_ADDRESS` with the parent beacon block root; a block without a parent beacon block root MUST be invalid, as EIP-4788 defines;
  3. in the block in which Satin activates, and in no other, check the EIP-7997 factory, as [Activation and Rex-Era Carry-Over](activation.md) specifies (`SATIN-ACT-005`);
  4. deploy the Oracle, the High-Precision Timestamp, KeylessDeploy, MegaAccessControl and MegaLimitControl, in that order, each as a state of its own ([System Contracts](../system-contracts/overview.md), `SATIN-SYSC-003`);
  5. read whether the SequencerRegistry has a role change due in the block (`SATIN-PBLK-004`), and when it has, call `applyPendingChanges()` on it;
  6. deploy the SequencerRegistry ([SequencerRegistry](../system-contracts/sequencer-registry.md), `SATIN-REG-008`);
  7. resolve the block's live system address from the registry (`SATIN-REG-002`).

  A block whose number is zero, the genesis block, MUST make no EIP-2935 call and no EIP-4788 call, and its parent beacon block root MUST be zero, as those EIPs define; it makes the other steps as every block does.
  Each step's state MUST be committed on its own, in that order — each deployment's, and the read-only entries of steps 3 to 7, included — before the next step and before the block's first transaction.

SATIN-POOL-022.text:
- `SATIN-POOL-022` A node MUST apply these departures from EIP-8037, each specified where it is linked:
  - the reservoir share (`SATIN-POOL-011`), where EIP-8037 passes the whole reservoir;
  - history gas, with a ledger of its own ([History Gas and the History Allowance](history-gas.md)); the history gas of the transaction's body is the intrinsic state gas;
  - the destruction-table refill (`SATIN-SD-004`, `SATIN-SD-005`, [SELFDESTRUCT and the Destruction Table](selfdestruct.md)), where EIP-8037 specifies that this destruction produces no state-gas refill;
  - the give-back rule (`SATIN-HIST-012`, `SATIN-POOL-012`, `SATIN-POOL-014`), where EIP-8037 at that commit credits a refill to regular gas first, up to the state gas the frame's regular gas paid (its outstanding spill, in last-in, first-out order), unwinds that spill when a child returns reservoir, and returns it to regular gas when the frame reverts;
  - the legacy share (`SATIN-STATE-012`).

  The last two follow the rule [ethereum/EIPs#12581](https://github.com/ethereum/EIPs/pull/12581) proposes for EIP-8037 (head `2ba9176f` when read, not merged), restated here in Satin's own words; the pull request is their provenance, not a rule of this specification.

SATIN-REG-001.text:
- `SATIN-REG-001` The SequencerRegistry MUST be the account at `SEQUENCER_REGISTRY_ADDRESS` holding the code of version 2.0.0 (`SEQUENCER_REGISTRY_CODE_HASH`, see [System Contracts](overview.md)), which has no interceptor: every call to it runs its bytecode.
  Its storage layout, interface and behaviour are the following.

  **Storage layout.**

| Slot | Name                            | Type             |
| ---- | ------------------------------- | ---------------- |
| 0    | `_currentSystemAddress`         | `address`        |
| 1    | `_currentSequencer`             | `address`        |
| 2    | `_admin`                        | `address`        |
| 3    | `_pendingAdmin`                 | `address`        |
| 4    | `_initialSystemAddress`         | `address`        |
| 5    | `_initialSequencer`             | `address`        |
| 6    | `_initialFromBlock`             | `uint256`        |
| 7    | `_pendingSystemAddress`         | `address`        |
| 8    | `_systemAddressActivationBlock` | `uint256`        |
| 9    | `_pendingSequencer`             | `address`        |
| 10   | `_sequencerActivationBlock`     | `uint256`        |
| 11   | `_systemAddressHistory`         | `ChangeRecord[]` |
| 12   | `_sequencerHistory`             | `ChangeRecord[]` |
| 13   | `_minRotationDelay`             | `uint256`        |

  A `ChangeRecord` is packed in one slot as `uint96 fromBlock` followed by `address addr`.
  Slots 0 to 12 are those of version 1.0.0; slot 13 is version 2.0.0's.
  A later version of the contract MUST only append storage slots after slot 13: it MUST NOT reorder the slots above or insert a slot before slot 14, since the keys of the two history arrays derive from their slots.

  **Interface.**

```solidity
interface ISequencerRegistry {
    struct ChangeRecord { uint96 fromBlock; address addr; }

    function currentSystemAddress() external view returns (address);
    function systemAddressAt(uint256 blockNumber) external view returns (address);
    function scheduleNextSystemAddressChange(address newSystemAddress, uint256 activationBlock) external;
    event SystemAddressChangeScheduled(address indexed oldSystemAddress, address indexed newSystemAddress, uint256 activationBlock);

    function currentSequencer() external view returns (address);
    function sequencerAt(uint256 blockNumber) external view returns (address);
    function scheduleNextSequencerChange(address newSequencer, uint256 activationBlock, bytes calldata newSequencerSignature) external;
    event SequencerChangeScheduled(address indexed oldSequencer, address indexed newSequencer, uint256 activationBlock);

    function minRotationDelay() external view returns (uint256);
    function rotationDigest(address newSequencer, uint256 activationBlock) external view returns (bytes32);

    function applyPendingChanges() external;
    function admin() external view returns (address);
    function pendingAdmin() external view returns (address);
    function transferAdmin(address newAdmin) external;
    function acceptAdmin() external;
    event AdminTransferStarted(address indexed currentAdmin, address indexed newPendingAdmin);
    event AdminTransferred(address indexed oldAdmin, address indexed newAdmin);

    function version() external view returns (string memory); // "2.0.0"

    error FutureBlock();
    error BeforeInitialBlock();
    error NotAdmin();
    error NotPendingAdmin();
    error ZeroAddress();
    error InvalidActivationBlock();
    error ActivationBlockTooLarge();
    error RotationDelayTooShort();
    error InvalidRotationProof();
}
```

  **Reads.** `currentSystemAddress()` and `currentSequencer()` return slots 0 and 1, which only `applyPendingChanges()` updates.
  `systemAddressAt(n)` and `sequencerAt(n)` return the role's address active at block `n`, from its history; they revert with `FutureBlock()` when `n` is above the current block number and with `BeforeInitialBlock()` when `n` is below `_initialFromBlock`, which both roles share.

  **Scheduling.** Only the admin may schedule a change (`NotAdmin()` otherwise).
  Each role has at most one pending change; a new schedule overwrites it.
  Both scheduling methods first require the activation block to be above the current block number (`InvalidActivationBlock()`).
  An activation block of `type(uint256).max` then cancels the role's pending change: the new address MUST be zero, and a non-zero one reverts with `ZeroAddress()`; a sequencer change's signature is ignored; and no other check is made, the `uint96` bound included, so `(0x11…11, type(uint256).max)` reverts with `ZeroAddress()` and not `ActivationBlockTooLarge()`.
  Any other activation block MUST fit in a `uint96` (`ActivationBlockTooLarge()`), and the new address MUST be non-zero (`ZeroAddress()`).
  A sequencer change MUST further satisfy `activationBlock ≥ block.number + minRotationDelay()` (`RotationDelayTooShort()`) and carry `newSequencerSignature`, a 65-byte `r ‖ s ‖ v` signature by the new sequencer's key over `rotationDigest(newSequencer, activationBlock)`, with `s` at most half the secp256k1 order and `v` 27 or 28, recovering to `newSequencer` (`InvalidRotationProof()` otherwise).
  The checks of a sequencer change are made in the order `InvalidActivationBlock`, the cancellation, `ActivationBlockTooLarge`, `ZeroAddress`, `RotationDelayTooShort`, the signature.
  `rotationDigest` is the [EIP-712](https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-712) digest `keccak256(0x1901 ‖ domainSeparator ‖ keccak256(abi.encode(SEQUENCER_ROTATION_TYPEHASH, newSequencer, activationBlock)))` with the domain `name = ROTATION_DOMAIN_NAME`, `version = ROTATION_DOMAIN_VERSION`, `chainId = block.chainid`, `verifyingContract` the registry, the domain separator computed at each call.

  **Applying.** `applyPendingChanges()` is permissionless: for each role whose pending change is due — a non-zero pending address whose activation block is at most the current block number — it sets the current address, appends a `ChangeRecord` to the role's history and clears the pending slots, both roles in one call.

  **Admin transfer.** `transferAdmin(newAdmin)`, by the admin, sets `_pendingAdmin` (zero cancels) and emits `AdminTransferStarted`; the admin's authority is unchanged until `acceptAdmin()`, by the pending admin alone (`NotPendingAdmin()` otherwise), sets `_admin` to the caller, clears `_pendingAdmin` and emits `AdminTransferred`.

SATIN-REG-008.text:
- `SATIN-REG-008` Every block MUST deploy the registry as a step of its pre-block sequence, after the due-change read and the `applyPendingChanges()` call (see [System Calls and the Pre-Block Sequence](../block/system-calls.md)), by what the state then holds at `SEQUENCER_REGISTRY_ADDRESS`:
  - an account whose code hash is `SEQUENCER_REGISTRY_CODE_HASH` (version 2.0.0) MUST be left as it is, and recorded as a read-only entry;
  - an account whose code hash is `SEQUENCER_REGISTRY_V1_CODE_HASH` (version 1.0.0) MUST be migrated in place: its code replaced by version 2.0.0, slot 13 (`_minRotationDelay`) written with the configured minimum rotation delay, and every other slot — the roles, the pending changes, the histories — kept, as its nonce and balance are;
  - an address with no account, or an account without code, MUST receive version 2.0.0 as a new contract, an existing account's nonce and balance kept and its storage cleared, and its storage seeded with: slots 0 (`_currentSystemAddress`) and 4 (`_initialSystemAddress`) the address `MEGA_SYSTEM_ADDRESS`; slots 1 (`_currentSequencer`) and 5 (`_initialSequencer`) the configured initial sequencer; slot 2 (`_admin`) the configured initial admin; slot 6 (`_initialFromBlock`) the block's number; and slot 13 (`_minRotationDelay`) the configured minimum rotation delay;
  - an account holding any other code MUST make the block invalid: the registry's storage is never overwritten by code it was not migrated to.

  The step writes nothing else, is charged nothing and counts toward none of the block's counters.

SATIN-SCHED-008.text:
- `SATIN-SCHED-008` The state-dependent parts of a transaction's cost MUST be charged at the start of its execution, before its first frame, as EIP-2780 and EIP-8037 specify, every state charge priced as [State Gas and SALT Pricing](state-gas.md) says, with no legacy share (`SATIN-POOL-024`), and every new-account charge decided by the predicates of `SATIN-STATE-011` (an authority by existence, every other account by EIP-161 emptiness), in this order:
  1. for a deposit, a Mega System Transaction executed as one included, whose caller is empty under EIP-161: the new-account state gas of that account, exactly once (`SATIN-DEP-003`, [Deposit Transactions](../block/deposits.md));
  2. for each authorization the list applies, in list order ([EIP-7702 Authorizations Before the First Frame](authorizations.md)): the new-account state gas of an authority the state holds no account for, then `ACCOUNT_WRITE` for the authority's first write, then the delegation indicator's state gas at most once per authority;
  3. the history gas of the write records made outside every frame: the applied authorities' records and the record of the first frame's start ([History Gas and the History Allowance](history-gas.md));
  4. the new-account state gas of the transaction's recipient, when its value goes to a recipient empty under EIP-161, or of its created account, when the created address is empty under EIP-161;
  5. for a call whose recipient holds an EIP-7702 delegation indicator: the delegate's access, in regular gas, `WARM_STORAGE_READ_COST` first and then, when the delegate is not warm, the additional cold account access of `SATIN-SCHED-003`, `COLD_ACCOUNT_ACCESS − WARM_STORAGE_READ_COST`.

  In general, every charge the base layer makes before the first frame stands, at the place and in the order the base layer gives it, unless a rule of the Satin section changes it (`SATIN-BASE-001`); step 5 is one of them, the access EIP-2780 charges for the code a delegated recipient runs.
  The delegate MUST be loaded at step 5 and at no earlier step, once `WARM_STORAGE_READ_COST` is paid, and the load makes it warm; a delegate that is not warm MUST be loaded only when the regular gas then left can pay the additional cold access, and a transaction that cannot pay it runs out of gas with the delegate not loaded (`SATIN-SCHED-009`).
  `ACCOUNT_WRITE` MUST NOT be charged for an authority whose account write the transaction has already paid: the sender, whose write validation pays in `TX_BASE_COST`; the recipient of a transaction that carries a non-zero value, whose write `TX_VALUE_COST` pays; and an authority an earlier applied authorization of the list already wrote.
  The load made for the first frame's start — of the recipient, or of the created address — MUST be made at step 4 and at no earlier step, so a transaction that cannot pay a charge of steps 1 to 3 makes no load for that start.
  An account the transaction loads earlier in another role — the sender at validation, an authority by the list's simulation and application (`SATIN-AUTH-003`, `SATIN-AUTH-005`) — is loaded all the same, and stays in the transaction's read set when it is also the recipient ([The Stateless Witness](../block/stateless-witness.md)).

SATIN-SD-019.text:
- `SATIN-SD-019` A `SELFDESTRUCT` MUST proceed in this order:
  1. its charges: its regular gas, at which compute is decided — a regular charge that crosses gas detention stops the frame there, and the operation applies none of the table and no beneficiary ([Gas Detention](gas-detention.md)) — and the new-account state gas of a beneficiary it creates, its legacy share first (16,000 at the current prices, `SATIN-STATE-012`); a charge the frame cannot pay halts the opcode before anything below;
  2. the table's give-back: the state-growth falls, the state-gas refills, the write records, and the deployed code's data size and history gas;
  3. its positive effects, held in the dimension order data size, KV updates, state growth (`SATIN-LIM-010`): the records it makes — the account it clears, when that account was not created in the transaction and this write makes its record; a loaded pre-existing slot of an account the table covers, when the deletion makes that slot's record; and its beneficiary — and its transfer log, then the beneficiary's state growth;
  4. the history of the records it makes (`SATIN-HIST-013`).

  When a positive effect crosses a limit, the frame stops and the operation keeps nothing: the table's effects, the beneficiary and the transfer log are discarded with it.

SATIN-SIZE-005.text:
- `SATIN-SIZE-005` `CREATE` and `CREATE2` MUST make their checks and charges in this order, and a node MUST add no charge ahead of them:
  1. the static context;
  2. the value, offset and size operands, and the size's range;
  3. the initcode size against `MAX_INITCODE_SIZE`;
  4. EIP-3860's initcode cost;
  5. the offset's range and the memory the initcode is read from, neither checked for empty initcode;
  6. for `CREATE2`, the salt operand, then `CREATE_ACCESS` and the hashing cost of the initcode; for `CREATE`, `CREATE_ACCESS`.

  The charges the creation makes for its start follow these ([History Gas and the History Allowance](history-gas.md)).

SATIN-STATE-004.text:
- `SATIN-STATE-004` A state charge MUST be made where the state is added:
  - a fresh slot, by the `SSTORE` that fills it (`SATIN-STATE-008`);
  - a new account, by the `CALL` that moves value to an account empty under EIP-161, by a `SELFDESTRUCT` that moves a non-zero balance to a distinct account empty under EIP-161, by the transaction whose value goes to a recipient empty under EIP-161, by an applied EIP-7702 authorization whose authority the state holds no account for, by a deposit or a Mega System Transaction whose caller is empty under EIP-161 ([Deposit Transactions](../block/deposits.md)), and by a keyless deployment whose signer's account is empty under EIP-161 ([KeylessDeploy](../system-contracts/keyless-deploy.md));
  - a created account, by `CREATE`, `CREATE2` or a creation transaction, when the created address is empty under EIP-161, and by a keyless deployment's creation ([KeylessDeploy](../system-contracts/keyless-deploy.md));
  - deployed code, at the code deposit ([Gas Schedule and Intrinsic Gas](gas-schedule.md));
  - a delegation indicator, by the applied authorization that writes it ([EIP-7702 Authorizations Before the First Frame](authorizations.md)).

  When each of these is charged relative to the opcode's other charges is specified on [History Gas and the History Allowance](history-gas.md) and [Gas Schedule and Intrinsic Gas](gas-schedule.md); how it draws the pools is `SATIN-POOL-008` ([Two-Pool Gas](gas-model.md)).

SATIN-TAKE-001.text:
- `SATIN-TAKE-001` For every pattern of the tables below, a node MUST charge, give back and count as the pattern's row states, in the order the row states, on each of these:
  - **regular gas and the EIP-3529 refund counter** — the regular charges of the opcodes ([Gas Schedule and Intrinsic Gas](gas-schedule.md)), and the refund counter the inherited `SSTORE` rules raise and lower, which is capped and applied only when the transaction settles (`SATIN-POOL-018`);
  - **state gas** — a state charge and its refill ([State Gas and SALT Pricing](state-gas.md)), at the bucket multiplier `m` fixed for the transaction (`SATIN-STATE-006`), a charge's legacy share (`SATIN-STATE-012`) being state gas that regular gas pays;
  - **history gas** — a history charge and its give-back ([History Gas and the History Allowance](history-gas.md));
  - **state growth**, **KV updates** and **data size** — the three counts of [Resource Accounting](resource-accounting.md), on the net of the frame named.

SATIN-VAL-002.text:
- `SATIN-VAL-002` A transaction MUST be rejected:
  - when its gas limit is below its intrinsic regular gas plus the history gas of its body (`CallGasCostMoreThanGasLimit`);
  - when its floor — the [EIP-7623](https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-7623) floor as Amsterdam computes it — exceeds its gas limit (`GasFloorMoreThanGasLimit`);
  - when its gas limit exceeds `EXECUTION_CAP` while its intrinsic regular gas or its floor exceeds `EXECUTION_CAP` (`GasFloorMoreThanGasLimit`).

  The intrinsic regular gas and the floor are those of [Gas Schedule and Intrinsic Gas](../evm/gas-schedule.md); the body's history gas is that of [History Gas](../evm/history-gas.md).

SATIN-VAL-003.text:
- `SATIN-VAL-003` Satin's own checks MUST be made in this order, and the first that fails names the transaction's refusal:
  1. the body with its authorization records against the data-size limit, before any gas is looked at — an invalid transaction whose reason is that the body and authorization records exceed the transaction data-size limit;
  2. the gas limit against the intrinsic regular gas alone — `CallGasCostMoreThanGasLimit`, carrying the intrinsic regular gas;
  3. the floor against the gas limit — `GasFloorMoreThanGasLimit`;
  4. the execution cap — `GasFloorMoreThanGasLimit`, carrying the larger of the intrinsic regular gas and the floor, against `EXECUTION_CAP`;
  5. the gas limit against the intrinsic regular gas plus the body's history gas — `CallGasCostMoreThanGasLimit`, carrying that sum.

  So a gas limit that covers the intrinsic regular gas but neither the floor nor the body's history gas is refused for the floor.
  Where a transaction also fails a check of the base layer, or the system-shape or chain-id check, which of the failures the node reports is not specified: only that the transaction is refused.

SATIN-VAL-010.text:
- `SATIN-VAL-010` A read a node fails to make for a transaction MUST be reported by its cause, and the transaction MUST NOT be settled, as a failed deposit or otherwise:
  - a failed bucket-capacity read, always as an internal error of the node (`SATIN-STATE-007`);
  - a failed database read the transaction's execution makes — in its validation, a Mega System Transaction's sender checks and the L1 block contract's account and slots its fee computation reads (`SATIN-WIT-011`) among them, in a frame, in the authorization list's simulation or application, or in a charge made before the first frame — as an internal error when the node's database declares the error fatal, and otherwise as an invalid block, as for a pre-block call (`SATIN-PBLK-006`);
  - a failed read the block's execution makes for the transaction outside its execution — the sender's account before the transaction runs, the L1 block contract's account and the footprint gas scalar the block reads before a non-deposit transaction — always as an internal error, as one in the factory check or in the resolution of the live system address is (`SATIN-REG-007`).

  A node that meets such a failure reaches no verdict on the block except the invalid block of the second case, and cannot execute the block past that transaction.

SATIN-WIT-001.text:
- `SATIN-WIT-001` A block's witness MUST hold:
  - every account and every storage slot the block loads, in a pre-block step or in a transaction the block includes, as the chain held it before the block: an absent account recorded as absent, an empty slot of an account the chain holds recorded as zero, and no slot of an absent account;
  - the code the chain held before the block for every such account, whether or not the load that named the account loaded its code;
  - the hash of every block `BLOCKHASH` read during the block's execution (`SATIN-WIT-014`);
  - the capacity of every SALT bucket the block's execution exports (`SATIN-WIT-013`).

  It holds no answer of the oracle service: every value an Oracle read of the block pushes is in the Oracle's slot at that read (see [Oracle](../system-contracts/oracle.md), `SATIN-ORCL-005`, `SATIN-ORCL-012`, `SATIN-ORCL-013`).
